  "body": {
    "id": "prop:three-node-contrast",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Set \\(p=3\\) and fix the ordered embedding \\(\\iota(X)=i_X=1\\), \\(\\iota(Y)=i_Y=2\\), and \\(\\iota(Z)=i_Z=3\\). For each type \\(r\\) in the fixed synthetic protocol, the truncated-factorization contrasts satisfy \\(\\tau_{i_Y,r}=3\\) and \\(\\tau_{i_Z,r}=25\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "dimension-genes",
      "designated-coordinate-embedding",
      "effect-vector",
      "witness-parameters",
      "dimension-types"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:three-designated-coordinates",
      "def:truncated-factorization-effect",
      "def:fixed-protocol"
    ],
    "justification": "This checks the designated-coordinate intervention arithmetic in the synthetic three-node application.",
    "gap": "The calculation is an application check and is not presented as a theorem-level novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "It supplies auditable contrast targets for the finite-input certificate without treating the synthetic box as biological calibration.",
    "proof_tex": "Under the fixed protocol and an intervention \\(V_1=x\\), the second node has\n\\[\nV_2\\mid\\operatorname{do}(V_1=x)\\sim\n\\operatorname{Pois}(\\lambda_x),\\qquad \\lambda_x=1+x^2.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[V_2\\mid\\operatorname{do}(V_1=x)]=\\lambda_x.\n\\]\nThe third conditional mean is \\(1+x+V_2^2\\).  Since a Poisson variable of mean \\(\\lambda\\) has second moment \\(\\lambda+\\lambda^2\\), iterated expectation gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[V_3\\mid\\operatorname{do}(V_1=x)]\n=1+x+\\lambda_x+\\lambda_x^2. \\tag{14}\n\\]\nAt \\(x=1\\), the two displayed means are \\(2\\) and \\(8\\); at \\(x=2\\), they are \\(5\\) and \\(33\\).  Subtracting the \\(x=1\\) values from the \\(x=2\\) values gives\n\\[\n\\tau_{i_Y,r}=5-2=3,\\qquad\n\\tau_{i_Z,r}=33-8=25.\n\\]\nThe conditional kernels other than that of the intervened coordinate are exactly those retained by the declared truncated factorization, so these are the causal contrasts in the proposition for each type.",
